  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAn MCS that moved into central West Virginia Sunday \nevening will provide a shield of stratiform light to moderate rain \nwith embedded thunder across eastern Ohio overnight Sunday night. \nWhile true flash flood concerns are lower, some localized heavier \nrates are tracking into southeastern Ohio and will bear monitoring \nfor a flash flood threat. Otherwise, longer-duration higher \namounts atop an already saturated ground that received 2-3\" in \nthe past 24 hours from eastern OH to southwestern PA could \nresult in rises on creeks and streams and an areal flood \nconcern. Come Monday morning, we'll have to monitor a few \nfeatures for a continued flooding threat. A slowly meandering \nboundary is progged by 00z guidance to park right around the \nMason Dixon Line through the day as another wave rides along it,\nand the strongest convergence/overrunning would support the \nbulk of the rain in northern WV and far southwestern PA in the \nmorning. The 00z HREF 6 hour mean through 10am paints 0.5- 0.75\"\nalong the I-70 corridor while the LPPM is around 2-3x that \ntotal which suggests that some convective enhancement is \npossible, so headed into the morning hours on Monday, focus will\nshift to that area. The 00z CAMs have exhibited a slight shift \nto the north in the boundary location, so if this footprint of \nQPF was to shift one way or the other, would think that a slight\nnortherly jog is in play, though still remaining south of \nPittsburgh. Given the very saturated ground already in place, \nand ensemble mean PWAT values at 1.7-1.9\", a Flood Watch remains\nin effect through noon Monday.\n\nCAMs then suggest another general lull Monday afternoon into the \nevening, and this is why the Flood Watch only extends through noon \nfor now. Another impulse arrives late Monday evening as does weak \nlow pressure riding along the surface boundary, and both features \nlook to reinvigorate showers and storms again, this time along the \nridges (best orographic ascent on the eastern side with a \nsoutheasterly wind) and north of Pittsburgh. Ensemble probability \nfor >1\" of rain then from Monday morning through Tuesday morning \nreaches 60-80% south and east of Pittsburgh with a finger of lower, \nbut not zero, probability extending back from Indiana to Butler \nCounty. The probability of >2\" is up to around 40% and >3\" around \n20% as well southeast of Pittsburgh, so higher end totals in that \narea are certainly on the table. That said, the flashy nature to the \nrainfall is still lower probability as the chance for >1\"/hour rates \nin any given hour in that same timeframe doesn't reach much higher \nthan 20%. In any case, flood threats will be maintained through at \nleast Wednesday.\n\nAnother upper trough arrives Tuesday into Wednesday, and ensemble \nmeans close this one off into a broad low which means we won't lose \nthe support for continued rain until likely Thursday when it exits \nand surface high pressure centered over New England establishes and \nkicks the surface boundary out. With this round, the higher totals \nare painted across eastern Ohio where NBM mean 24 hour amounts \nthrough 8 PM Wednesday sit around 0.75-1\" but still suggest the \npotential for higher amounts with the 75th to 90th percentiles of \n1.5-2\". Some showers may even linger into Thursday as the main \napproaching trough possibly closes off into an upper low. There is \nstill hope for a drier pattern for Friday and next weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCool temperatures remain the second part of the story this week.\nGiven the abundant cloud cover and stubborn east/northeast flow, \nhigher ridge locations may have trouble reaching 60 degrees Tuesday \nthrough Thursday, while lower and mid 60s will be common west of the \nhigher elevations. While minimum temperatures will remain fairly \nclose to normal, daytime highs may run some 10 degrees below \nclimatology during the midweek period.",
